              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "When receiving food deliveries in a commercial food service setting, the safe temperatures at which food can be accepted depend on the type of food. \n\nThe general standards set by the **FDA Food Code** and **ServSafe** are detailed below:\n\n### 1. Cold TCS (Time/Temperature Control for Safety) Food\n* **Standard:** Must be received at **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower**.\n* *Examples:* Fresh meat, poultry, fish, pre-cut produce, and pre-packaged cold items.\n\n### 2. Hot TCS Food\n* **Standard:** Must be received at **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**.\n* *Examples:* Prepared meals, hot catering deliveries, or soups.\n\n### 3. Frozen Food\n* **Standard:** Must be received **frozen solid**. \n* **When to Reject:** Reject any frozen food if there are fluid stains on the packaging, dampness, or visible **ice crystals** (which indicate the food has thawed and refrozen).\n\n---\n\n### Special Exceptions \nA few specific cold items are legally allowed to be received at slightly higher temperatures, provided they are cooled down quickly:\n\n* **Milk:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but it must be cooled to **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours**.\n* **Shell Eggs:** Can be received at an air temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**.\n* **Shucked Shellfish:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but must be cooled to **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours**.\n* **Live Shellfish (Oysters, Clams, Mussels, Scallops):** Can be received at an air temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** and an internal temperature no greater than **50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C)**. Once received, they must be cooled to **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours**.\n\n### Tips for Receiving Food Safely\n* **Always use a calibrated thermometer:** Check the internal temperature of meats, poultry, and fish by inserting the thermometer probe into the thickest part of the food. \n* **Check Reduced Oxygen Packaging (ROP):** For vacuum-packed or sous-vide foods, fold the packaging around the thermometer probe rather than puncturing it.\n* **Look at quality:** Reject food that is moldy, has an abnormal color, smells unpleasant, or shows signs of pest damage.",
              "thought": null,
